    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The stone carries one priestly decree in three scripts, the language of the gods, of documents and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, with only minor differences between them.</li><li>Its decipherment was the work of many scholars over two decades, from Silvestre de Sacy and Åkerblad on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script">Demotic</a> to Young and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Champollion</a> in 1822.</li><li>Taken by the British from the French in 1801 and shown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> ever since, it has been the subject of Egyptian requests for its return since 2003.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The stone's dark colour, which led to its long identification as black basalt, came from a layer of carnauba wax added in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>; cleaning in 1999 revealed dark grey granodiorite with a pink vein.</p></aside>
    </section>

